From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: dcomp: a device, window targets and visuals

DCompositionCreateDevice was a stub, and WebView2's GPU process CHECKs
that it succeeds (a breakpoint, then the GPU process restarts until
Chromium gives up; nothing is ever painted).

This is the part of DirectComposition that shows a composition swap chain
in a window: IDCompositionDevice (version 1) with CreateTargetForHwnd,
CreateVisual and Commit; IDCompositionTarget::SetRoot; IDCompositionVisual
with offsets, content and child visuals. Commit gives each composition swap
chain in a target's tree the target window and the visual's offset (patch
0190's IWineDXGICompositionSwapChain).

Not done: surfaces, transforms, clips, effects, animations. The version 2
and 3 devices still fail, on purpose: Chromium probes for those to decide
whether to use DirectComposition for GPU compositing, and keeps its plain
window path when they are absent.

include/dcomp.idl had IDCompositionVisual's overloaded methods in the wrong
vtable order. MSVC lays out an overload set in reverse declaration order,
so SetOffsetX(IDCompositionAnimation*) precedes SetOffsetX(float), and
likewise for SetOffsetY, SetTransform and SetClip (mingw-w64's dcomp.h
encodes the same order). The other interfaces there with overloads
(transforms) have the same problem and are left alone; nothing implements
them.

